  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2x+2y+{y}^{2}+1=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+2y+{y}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2x)+({y}^{2}+2y) & =-1 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =-1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={1}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-1,-1)$ and its radius is $1.$",
